1
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:06,300
This is part one of a two-part module covering radiographic anatomy of the chest.

2
00:00:08,650 --> 00:00:12,070
Following completion of both part one and part two of this module,

3
00:00:12,070 --> 00:00:20,690
the learner should be able to identify normal anatomic structures on both the frontal and lateral view of a chest radiograph.

4
00:00:21,390 --> 00:00:25,650
As a way of review, remember there are five radiographic densities.

5
00:00:26,670 --> 00:00:31,250
Interfaces are formed when structures of different density contact one another,

6
00:00:31,250 --> 00:00:36,070
so we can think of a radiograph, in this example, a frontal view of the chest,

7
00:00:36,330 --> 00:00:39,830
as simply representing a pattern of these interfaces.

8
00:00:40,530 --> 00:00:48,810
We're going to start this discussion by identifying the interfaces that make up the normal borders of the mediastinum on the frontal view.

9
00:00:50,850 --> 00:00:56,330
This is a coned-down view of the mediastinum on a frontal image of the chest.

10
00:00:56,830 --> 00:00:59,050
We're going to begin our discussion on the right.

11
00:00:59,050 --> 00:01:04,130
The upper border on the right side of the mediastinum

12
00:01:04,130 --> 00:01:09,790
is made up of the interface between the superior vena cava and the adjacent lung.

13
00:01:10,750 --> 00:01:15,870
The right heart border on the frontal view is made up of the right atrium as it contacts the middle lobe.

14
00:01:15,870 --> 00:01:20,370
On the left, we're going to have three major interfaces.

15
00:01:20,950 --> 00:01:28,630
The most cranial would be of the aortic arch, followed by the main pulmonary artery, and finally the left ventricle.

16
00:01:28,630 --> 00:01:37,970
We can also infer the presence of the left atrial appendage somewhere just below the main pulmonary artery.

17
00:01:39,150 --> 00:01:44,230
Now, usually you can't really identify it as a discrete structure unless it's enlarged,

18
00:01:44,430 --> 00:01:50,470
in which case it forms sort of a fourth bump on the left side of the mediastinum.

19
00:01:50,470 --> 00:01:57,290
However, this still represents a normal border-forming structure on the frontal view.

20
00:01:58,750 --> 00:02:03,290
Moving on to the hilum, on the left side, we're going to have the left pulmonary artery,

21
00:02:03,290 --> 00:02:08,330
which forms the majority of the opacity of the left hilum,

22
00:02:08,890 --> 00:02:15,650
followed by the right pulmonary artery and the right interlobar pulmonary artery.

23
00:02:15,650 --> 00:02:22,660
Notice that the left hilum is higher than the right hilum, which is a normal finding

24
00:02:22,660 --> 00:02:25,400
because of the anatomy of the left pulmonary artery,

25
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:32,780
which arches over the left upper lobe bronchus as it goes anterior to posterior

26
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:35,700
as it branches from the main pulmonary artery.

27
00:02:36,060 --> 00:02:40,980
So the left hilum will typically be, or will normally be, higher than the right.

28
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:45,940
We can use cross-sectional anatomy, in this case CT,

29
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:50,480
to help us understand the borders that we just identified on radiography.

30
00:02:50,920 --> 00:02:54,880
Now, remember, the borders are formed by interfaces between gas in the lung

31
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:57,060
and the mediastinal fat in soft tissues.

32
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:02,100
On the right side, cranially, we're going to have the superior vena cava

33
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:06,440
and more caudally, the right atrium.

34
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:09,500
On the left, we're going to have the aorta,

35
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:15,240
the most cranial bump, followed by the main pulmonary artery as we move caudally,

36
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:17,860
and then finally, the left ventricle.

37
00:03:18,920 --> 00:03:22,400
Notice how these structures contact the adjacent lung,

38
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:28,880
which gives you those interfaces that we're able to identify on the radiographs.

39
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:33,710
Now let's look at coronal reformatted CT images

40
00:03:33,710 --> 00:03:37,010
to also help us understand the radiographic borders.

41
00:03:37,590 --> 00:03:41,390
So we're going to have an image that is anterior on the left

42
00:03:41,390 --> 00:03:43,550
and then more posterior on the right.

43
00:03:44,010 --> 00:03:49,150
We can see the right upper mediastinal border formed by the superior vena cava

44
00:03:49,150 --> 00:03:55,270
and the lower heart border on the right is the right atrium.

45
00:03:56,550 --> 00:03:58,350
On the left, we're going to have the aorta,

46
00:03:58,870 --> 00:04:00,410
followed by the main pulmonary artery,

47
00:04:01,070 --> 00:04:03,550
and then followed by the left ventricle.

48
00:04:03,550 --> 00:04:09,790
Now we're going to continue to march posteriorly on the coronal views.

49
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:14,190
The image on the left represents the image from the prior slide

50
00:04:14,190 --> 00:04:17,730
where you can see the aorta, the main pulmonary artery,

51
00:04:17,870 --> 00:04:21,690
and the left ventricle forming the three major structures

52
00:04:21,690 --> 00:04:25,730
that create interfaces on the left mediastinum.

53
00:04:26,610 --> 00:04:32,110
The image on the right is an image that's coronal and posterior to the image on the left,

54
00:04:32,230 --> 00:04:35,610
and this is going to show us that fourth border forming structure.

55
00:04:36,310 --> 00:04:40,910
Here we can see projecting between the main pulmonary artery and the left ventricle

56
00:04:40,910 --> 00:04:45,870
this outpouching of the left atrium called the left atrial appendage.

57
00:04:46,310 --> 00:04:52,470
This will give us that final kind of fourth border forming structure on the left.

58
00:04:52,770 --> 00:04:55,310
Now notice that in terms of the heart border,

59
00:04:55,730 --> 00:05:01,170
the right heart border is comprised of the right atrium.

60
00:05:01,450 --> 00:05:05,770
On the left heart border, we're going to have the majority of that made up by the left ventricle,

61
00:05:05,870 --> 00:05:09,810
but also the component of the left atrium, which we talked about,

62
00:05:09,890 --> 00:05:11,750
which is called the left atrial appendage.

63
00:05:12,130 --> 00:05:16,210
Notice that the right ventricle does not form an interface with the lung

64
00:05:16,210 --> 00:05:18,330
on a frontal view of the chest.

65
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:24,840
Now let's take a little bit of a deeper dive into some of the mediastinal structures.

66
00:05:24,840 --> 00:05:28,520
Remember on the left, we're going to have the aortic arch,

67
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,100
and just coddled to this will be the main pulmonary artery.

68
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:41,100
Notice there is a concave medial recess between these two structures, as we see here.

69
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:47,080
This is termed the aortopulmonary window or a shortened version AP window.

70
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:50,600
So this should normally be a concavity.

71
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:59,200
Any convex contour of the AP window is abnormal and may reflect lymphadenopathy or other mediastinal masses.

72
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:03,360
Now we can turn our attention to the airways.

73
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:07,040
We can see the trachea just to the right of the midline

74
00:06:07,040 --> 00:06:13,160
and then branching off into the right and left main stem bronchi, as we see here.

75
00:06:13,700 --> 00:06:17,640
We can identify the carina, that branch point of the trachea,

76
00:06:17,780 --> 00:06:24,800
which will be an important landmark when we're trying to determine the correct positioning for endotracheal tubes.

77
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:30,420
And then finally, just above or cranial to the right main stem bronchus,

78
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:35,380
as we see the superior vuna cava kind of extending from cranial to caudal,

79
00:06:35,760 --> 00:06:40,360
we're going to see this circle, which represents the asicus vein.

80
00:06:40,700 --> 00:06:46,520
Now to understand this, I have an image of a CT on the right-hand side.

81
00:06:46,520 --> 00:06:51,180
Now remember, the asicus vein is a posterior mediastinal structure,

82
00:06:51,960 --> 00:06:56,220
but right above the right main stem bronchus,

83
00:06:56,820 --> 00:07:03,580
the asicus vein will travel from posterior to anterior to empty into the superior vena cava,

84
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:05,280
as we can see here.

85
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:10,140
So you can understand with this kind of posterior to anterior movement,

86
00:07:10,260 --> 00:07:11,840
right above the right main stem bronchus,

87
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:16,240
on a frontal view radiograph, the asicus vein is going to look like a circle.

88
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:19,220
Just cranial to the right main stem bronchus.

89
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:24,780
The cardioprenic fat pads may be present unilaterally or bilaterally.

90
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:28,220
It's important to identify these as normal structures,

91
00:07:28,640 --> 00:07:30,780
because they can be mistaken for pathology.

92
00:07:31,440 --> 00:07:33,840
For example, they can be mistaken as part of the heart,

93
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:36,020
in which case you may overhaul cardiomegaly,

94
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:40,020
or sometimes they can be mistaken for mediastinal mass or adenopathy.

95
00:07:40,020 --> 00:07:45,380
The key in identifying these structures are their location adjacent to the heart,

96
00:07:45,940 --> 00:07:47,360
their triangular shape,

97
00:07:47,820 --> 00:07:51,780
and the fact that they are slightly less dense than the heart,

98
00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:57,100
because this represents fat as opposed to the myocardium of the heart.

99
00:07:57,460 --> 00:07:59,000
Remember, fat is less dense.

100
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:04,700
So in this example, the left ventricular margin is indicated by the arrows.

101
00:08:05,720 --> 00:08:11,340
However, there is a triangular shaped opacity that extends more lateral than the left ventricular margin.

102
00:08:11,340 --> 00:08:14,780
This represents the left cardioprenic fat pad.

103
00:08:22,860 --> 00:08:24,440
On the frontal view of the chest,

104
00:08:24,960 --> 00:08:28,560
the right atrium forms the entire right heart border.

105
00:08:29,220 --> 00:08:30,420
On the left side,

106
00:08:30,960 --> 00:08:35,440
the left heart border consists of a small segment of the left atrium,

107
00:08:35,880 --> 00:08:37,420
termed the left atrial appendage,

108
00:08:37,420 --> 00:08:42,480
and the majority of the left heart border is comprised of the left ventricle.

109
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:45,820
The right ventricle is the most anterior heart chamber

110
00:08:45,820 --> 00:08:48,700
and is not border forming on the frontal view.

111
00:08:50,360 --> 00:08:54,120
We're now going to talk about some of the more commonly encountered interfaces

112
00:08:54,120 --> 00:08:56,880
that we will see on a frontal view of the chest.

113
00:08:57,920 --> 00:09:00,360
A stripe is formed by gas,

114
00:09:00,720 --> 00:09:02,600
such as within the lung or airways,

115
00:09:02,600 --> 00:09:07,760
that outlines thin intervening tissue between two gas-filled structures.

116
00:09:08,680 --> 00:09:11,600
So in this case, we see the right paratracheal stripe.

117
00:09:12,300 --> 00:09:16,780
Now this represents the visceral and parietal plura of the right upper lobe

118
00:09:16,780 --> 00:09:19,900
contacting the right lateral border of the trachea,

119
00:09:20,180 --> 00:09:23,960
as we can see on this CT image on our left.

120
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:27,780
So basically, we have gas within the lung on one side

121
00:09:27,780 --> 00:09:30,060
and gas within the trachea on the other side,

122
00:09:30,060 --> 00:09:35,100
and this is outlining this thin intervening tissue,

123
00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:39,240
which represents the plura and mediastinal fat in soft tissues.

124
00:09:39,780 --> 00:09:42,500
Now, how does this translate onto a radiograph?

125
00:09:43,040 --> 00:09:45,960
We have a radiograph on the right side.

126
00:09:46,560 --> 00:09:49,260
We can see along the right tracheal margin,

127
00:09:49,680 --> 00:09:53,120
this radio-opaque vertically oriented line,

128
00:09:53,760 --> 00:09:59,220
and that should be less than or equal to four millimeters for normal cases.

129
00:10:00,060 --> 00:10:02,540
That represents the right paratracheal stripe.

130
00:10:03,980 --> 00:10:10,720
The left paraspinal stripe is formed by the tangential contact of the left lung and plura

131
00:10:10,720 --> 00:10:15,500
with the posterior mediastinal fat and adjacent mediastinal soft tissues.

132
00:10:16,080 --> 00:10:20,340
This typically lies medial to the lateral wall of the descending thoracic aorta.

133
00:10:20,340 --> 00:10:23,660
So if we look at the CT on our right,

134
00:10:24,260 --> 00:10:26,860
we can see the lateral margin of the descending aorta.

135
00:10:27,480 --> 00:10:32,980
The corresponding radiographic interface is identified with the green arrows here.

136
00:10:33,560 --> 00:10:35,020
Now, if we go back to the CT,

137
00:10:35,740 --> 00:10:38,240
we can identify semidiastinal fat,

138
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:40,680
which forms a tangent with the x-ray beam,

139
00:10:41,220 --> 00:10:42,100
on a frontal view,

140
00:10:42,100 --> 00:10:47,060
which will give us a second interface to the left of the spine,

141
00:10:47,220 --> 00:10:54,840
which is more medially located than the lateral margin of the descending aorta.

142
00:10:55,160 --> 00:10:57,920
On the radiograph, we can see a second stripe,

143
00:10:58,280 --> 00:10:59,700
more medial.

144
00:10:59,700 --> 00:11:02,500
That represents the left paraspinal stripe.

145
00:11:03,020 --> 00:11:04,360
So if we're able to identify this,

146
00:11:04,420 --> 00:11:09,700
we want to make sure it's not too thick or abnormally convex outward,

147
00:11:10,180 --> 00:11:12,740
which may be a sign of an abnormal mass,

148
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:17,220
such as a hematoma related to an adjacent vertebral body fracture

149
00:11:17,220 --> 00:11:20,360
or a posterior mediastinal mass.

150
00:11:20,360 --> 00:11:27,400
The azagoesophageal recess refers to the interface between the right lower lobe

151
00:11:27,400 --> 00:11:32,340
and the adjacent mediastinum consisting of the esophagus and azagous vein.

152
00:11:33,240 --> 00:11:36,020
So let's take a look at the CT images on the left,

153
00:11:36,160 --> 00:11:38,360
starting in the bottom on lung windows.

154
00:11:38,700 --> 00:11:42,040
We can see this little recess of the right lower lobe

155
00:11:42,040 --> 00:11:46,400
that extends towards the midline and contacts the mediastinum.

156
00:11:46,400 --> 00:11:52,180
So we have that same image on the top in soft tissue windows

157
00:11:52,180 --> 00:11:54,700
to highlight the mediastinal soft tissues.

158
00:11:55,080 --> 00:11:58,140
You can see at this level,

159
00:11:58,260 --> 00:12:02,020
we're going to have the azagous vein as well as the esophagus.

160
00:12:02,500 --> 00:12:05,120
So that recess of the right lower lobe

161
00:12:05,120 --> 00:12:09,300
is what contacts the azagous vein and the esophagus

162
00:12:09,300 --> 00:12:12,440
and hence the term azagoesophageal recess.

163
00:12:13,180 --> 00:12:15,880
So what does this translate to on radiography?

164
00:12:16,420 --> 00:12:19,560
Well, notice behind the heart, kind of in the midline,

165
00:12:19,700 --> 00:12:21,660
we're going to have this obliquely oriented line

166
00:12:21,660 --> 00:12:24,720
and that's going to represent that interface

167
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:27,560
between the right lower lobe and the azagous vein

168
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:29,400
and esophagus of the mediastinum.

169
00:12:29,780 --> 00:12:31,620
So we can see that line here

170
00:12:31,620 --> 00:12:34,500
and I'll kind of take that line off

171
00:12:34,500 --> 00:12:37,940
and then hopefully you can identify that interface there

172
00:12:37,940 --> 00:12:40,220
between the right lower lobe and the mediastinum.

173
00:12:42,700 --> 00:12:47,580
Let's now talk about the soft tissues, diaphragm, and bones

174
00:12:47,580 --> 00:12:51,300
to round out our anatomy on the frontal view of the chest.

175
00:12:51,720 --> 00:12:54,020
I'm going to start in the midline of the chest

176
00:12:54,020 --> 00:12:55,140
looking at the bones.

177
00:12:55,500 --> 00:12:57,780
It's important to be able to recognize the manubrium

178
00:12:57,780 --> 00:12:59,500
of the sternum as a normal structure

179
00:12:59,500 --> 00:13:03,160
so that this is not mistaken as lymphadenopathy or mass.

180
00:13:03,160 --> 00:13:07,540
We're going to have the clavicle and the scapula

181
00:13:07,540 --> 00:13:09,760
so these should be symmetric bilaterally

182
00:13:09,760 --> 00:13:11,220
as well as the ribs.

183
00:13:14,140 --> 00:13:15,500
Moving on to the soft tissues,

184
00:13:15,680 --> 00:13:16,800
this is a female patient.

185
00:13:16,800 --> 00:13:20,100
we can see that there are symmetric breast shadows.

186
00:13:21,720 --> 00:13:23,380
We look at the diaphragm.

187
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:25,760
They are domed in appearance.

188
00:13:26,220 --> 00:13:27,820
We're at the apex of the dome,

189
00:13:27,980 --> 00:13:31,220
somewhere in the middle third of the chest.

190
00:13:31,740 --> 00:13:34,340
As the diaphragms move out laterally,

191
00:13:34,960 --> 00:13:38,100
they move caudally and will form these recesses

192
00:13:38,100 --> 00:13:41,380
where the diaphragm contacts the lateral margin

193
00:13:41,380 --> 00:13:42,200
of the chest wall.

194
00:13:42,840 --> 00:13:45,420
This is termed the lateral costophenic sulcus.

195
00:13:45,420 --> 00:13:48,680
And when we start talking about interpretation

196
00:13:48,680 --> 00:13:50,400
of chest radiographs,

197
00:13:50,500 --> 00:13:53,200
these sulci are going to be important to evaluate.

198
00:13:54,020 --> 00:13:57,920
We need to make sure that they form nice sharp margins

199
00:13:57,920 --> 00:14:01,340
and there's nothing like a mass or effusion

200
00:14:01,340 --> 00:14:04,220
or atelectasis or airspace disease

201
00:14:04,220 --> 00:14:07,300
that would cause blunting of these normally sharp

202
00:14:07,300 --> 00:14:09,300
lateral costophenic sulci.

203
00:14:09,800 --> 00:14:13,830
Let's take a little closer look at rib anatomy

204
00:14:13,830 --> 00:14:16,370
as this can sometimes be confusing.

205
00:14:16,790 --> 00:14:20,470
Now the ribs don't travel in the same horizontal plane

206
00:14:20,470 --> 00:14:24,930
so that in terms of the cranial caudal direction,

207
00:14:25,130 --> 00:14:28,030
the posterior rib will not be in the same plane

208
00:14:28,030 --> 00:14:29,650
as the anterior rib.

209
00:14:29,850 --> 00:14:32,490
The posterior ribs are more cranial

210
00:14:32,490 --> 00:14:34,630
than the corresponding anterior ribs.

211
00:14:34,630 --> 00:14:37,550
So if we take a look at an example

212
00:14:37,550 --> 00:14:40,190
where we outline one specific rib

213
00:14:40,190 --> 00:14:42,170
and follow it from posterior to anterior,

214
00:14:42,610 --> 00:14:44,890
we can see the anterior portion of that rib

215
00:14:44,890 --> 00:14:47,310
is more caudally located

216
00:14:47,310 --> 00:14:51,470
than the posterior portion of the rib.

217
00:14:51,930 --> 00:14:54,350
So the posterior ribs and the anterior ribs

218
00:14:54,350 --> 00:14:56,190
are going to have different orientations.

219
00:14:56,190 --> 00:15:00,530
I think the orientation of the ribs will be more clear

220
00:15:00,530 --> 00:15:02,990
if we do a side-by-side comparison.

221
00:15:03,910 --> 00:15:05,270
So we have the same image here.

222
00:15:05,430 --> 00:15:06,630
On the left-hand side,

223
00:15:06,710 --> 00:15:09,350
we're going to outline the posterior ribs

224
00:15:09,350 --> 00:15:12,010
and on the right-hand side, the anterior ribs.

225
00:15:12,870 --> 00:15:16,650
So the posterior ribs are more horizontally oriented

226
00:15:16,650 --> 00:15:18,210
as we see here.

227
00:15:18,210 --> 00:15:22,770
The anterior ribs are more obliquely oriented

228
00:15:22,770 --> 00:15:27,110
so that the ribs travel caudally and medially.

229
00:15:28,310 --> 00:15:30,270
So notice the difference in orientation

230
00:15:30,270 --> 00:15:33,350
between anterior and posterior ribs.

231
00:15:40,110 --> 00:15:42,510
The right and left portions of the diaphragm

232
00:15:42,510 --> 00:15:43,590
are outlined in yellow.

233
00:15:44,350 --> 00:15:46,050
Also indicated are the ribs.

234
00:15:46,930 --> 00:15:49,770
The costophenic sulci or costophenic angles

235
00:15:49,770 --> 00:15:52,290
are the recesses between the ribs

236
00:15:52,290 --> 00:15:55,470
and the lateral-most aspects of the diaphragm.

237
00:15:58,140 --> 00:15:59,900
We are now going to turn our attention

238
00:15:59,900 --> 00:16:02,100
to the normal mediastinal borders

239
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:03,880
on the lateral radiograph.

240
00:16:05,480 --> 00:16:08,480
Anteriorly, we're going to have the right ventricle

241
00:16:08,480 --> 00:16:10,700
and right ventricular outflow tract.

242
00:16:11,240 --> 00:16:12,780
Remember that the right ventricle

243
00:16:12,780 --> 00:16:14,860
is the most anterior cardiac chamber.

244
00:16:15,500 --> 00:16:18,440
We are then going to identify two arches.

245
00:16:18,840 --> 00:16:20,560
The larger, more cranial arch

246
00:16:20,560 --> 00:16:22,200
is going to represent the aorta.

247
00:16:22,200 --> 00:16:25,980
The smaller, more caudally located arch

248
00:16:25,980 --> 00:16:28,000
is the left pulmonary artery.

249
00:16:28,420 --> 00:16:30,340
Remember the course of the left pulmonary artery.

250
00:16:30,840 --> 00:16:33,920
It branches from the main pulmonary artery

251
00:16:33,920 --> 00:16:36,920
and courses anterior to posterior

252
00:16:36,920 --> 00:16:41,680
arching over the left upper lobe bronchus

253
00:16:41,680 --> 00:16:44,900
as identified by this oval-shaped lucency.

254
00:16:44,900 --> 00:16:48,400
Now, the right pulmonary artery

255
00:16:48,400 --> 00:16:51,680
is poorly defined on the lateral view

256
00:16:51,680 --> 00:16:55,740
and is not typically as easy to identify

257
00:16:55,740 --> 00:16:57,060
as the left pulmonary artery.

258
00:16:57,500 --> 00:16:59,500
This is because of the branching pattern

259
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:00,920
of the right pulmonary artery

260
00:17:00,920 --> 00:17:04,300
as well as the lack of any adjacent lung

261
00:17:04,300 --> 00:17:06,280
to give you a nice interface to identify.

262
00:17:06,280 --> 00:17:12,300
The right pulmonary artery can be inferred

263
00:17:12,300 --> 00:17:14,920
to be present right about this location

264
00:17:14,920 --> 00:17:15,680
as shown there.

265
00:17:16,900 --> 00:17:20,340
It's really better termed the hyalurvascular opacity

266
00:17:20,340 --> 00:17:23,300
because it really represents a conglomerate

267
00:17:23,300 --> 00:17:25,200
of pulmonary arteries and veins

268
00:17:25,200 --> 00:17:27,680
with the right pulmonary artery forming more

269
00:17:27,680 --> 00:17:31,140
of the cranial border and the interlobal artery

270
00:17:31,140 --> 00:17:32,940
as we saw on the frontal view

271
00:17:32,940 --> 00:17:36,480
forming more of the lower aspect of that opacity.

272
00:17:36,820 --> 00:17:38,740
So you're not going to see the right pulmonary artery

273
00:17:38,740 --> 00:17:40,200
typically as well as the left.

274
00:17:41,420 --> 00:17:45,960
Along the postero-superior margin of the heart

275
00:17:45,960 --> 00:17:47,420
we're going to have the left atrium.

276
00:17:47,680 --> 00:17:50,080
That's the most posteriorly located heart chamber.

277
00:17:50,880 --> 00:17:52,600
And then finally the left ventricle

278
00:17:52,600 --> 00:17:55,100
being located posterior and inferior.

279
00:17:55,100 --> 00:17:59,720
There are a couple more vascular structures

280
00:17:59,720 --> 00:18:02,000
that we need to identify on the lateral view.

281
00:18:02,980 --> 00:18:05,080
Remember the most posterior chamber

282
00:18:05,080 --> 00:18:06,280
is the left atrium

283
00:18:06,280 --> 00:18:08,860
forming the upper posterior margin of the heart.

284
00:18:09,740 --> 00:18:11,520
Notice we have these opacities

285
00:18:11,520 --> 00:18:14,380
which are coursing towards the left atrium.

286
00:18:15,120 --> 00:18:17,840
This represents the confluence of the pulmonary veins.

287
00:18:19,040 --> 00:18:21,080
And then finally sometimes we're able to identify

288
00:18:21,080 --> 00:18:22,720
the inferior vena cava

289
00:18:22,720 --> 00:18:26,720
as an interface between the diaphragm

290
00:18:26,720 --> 00:18:31,160
and caudal margin of the heart posteriorly.

291
00:18:32,280 --> 00:18:34,140
Here we see that interface

292
00:18:34,140 --> 00:18:36,140
between the inferior vena cava and lung.

293
00:18:38,320 --> 00:18:39,940
That's the inferior vena cava.

294
00:18:40,480 --> 00:18:44,740
Now let's turn our attention to the airways

295
00:18:44,740 --> 00:18:48,220
as identified on the lateral radiograph.

296
00:18:49,100 --> 00:18:50,380
First we're going to notice

297
00:18:50,380 --> 00:18:52,740
the vertically oriented air column

298
00:18:52,740 --> 00:18:54,500
that represents the trachea

299
00:18:54,500 --> 00:18:55,800
and the upper medius dynum.

300
00:18:59,250 --> 00:19:02,510
Notice along the posterior margin of the trachea

301
00:19:02,510 --> 00:19:04,610
we're going to have a radio opaque

302
00:19:04,610 --> 00:19:07,650
vertically oriented line.

303
00:19:07,810 --> 00:19:10,450
That's going to be the posterior tracheal stripe

304
00:19:10,450 --> 00:19:12,610
which we'll talk about in more detail

305
00:19:12,610 --> 00:19:14,290
a little bit later.

306
00:19:15,790 --> 00:19:18,250
We're going to have the right upper lobe bronchus

307
00:19:18,250 --> 00:19:22,310
and then the left main stem bronchus

308
00:19:22,310 --> 00:19:23,810
upper lobe continuum

309
00:19:23,810 --> 00:19:25,750
and I'll talk a little bit about this

310
00:19:25,750 --> 00:19:28,050
in later slides.

311
00:19:31,360 --> 00:19:32,900
As we did for the frontal view

312
00:19:32,900 --> 00:19:34,180
for the lateral view

313
00:19:34,180 --> 00:19:37,060
we're also going to look at cross-sectional imaging

314
00:19:37,060 --> 00:19:38,180
specifically CT

315
00:19:38,180 --> 00:19:41,260
to help us understand the radiographic findings.

316
00:19:41,260 --> 00:19:44,960
The right-sided image represents a coronal view.

317
00:19:46,280 --> 00:19:48,700
We can see the left pulmonary artery

318
00:19:48,700 --> 00:19:50,140
as it arches over

319
00:19:50,140 --> 00:19:54,060
the left main bronchus

320
00:19:54,060 --> 00:19:57,000
as well as the left upper lobe bronchus

321
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,300
and this really represents a continuum.

322
00:19:59,560 --> 00:20:00,260
So you can imagine

323
00:20:00,260 --> 00:20:01,760
based on this coronal anatomy

324
00:20:01,760 --> 00:20:04,140
if you're looking at the patient from the side

325
00:20:04,140 --> 00:20:06,120
like you would for a lateral radiograph

326
00:20:06,120 --> 00:20:08,260
this is going to be oval in shape

327
00:20:08,260 --> 00:20:09,960
and going to represent this continuum

328
00:20:09,960 --> 00:20:11,480
of the left main stem bronchus

329
00:20:11,480 --> 00:20:13,060
and the upper lobe bronchus.

330
00:20:13,640 --> 00:20:15,880
On a sagittal reconstructed view

331
00:20:15,880 --> 00:20:17,240
as we see on the left

332
00:20:17,240 --> 00:20:20,280
you can see the left upper lobe bronchus

333
00:20:20,280 --> 00:20:21,440
there is an oval shape

334
00:20:21,440 --> 00:20:22,520
which corresponds to

335
00:20:22,520 --> 00:20:23,960
what we see on the radiograph

336
00:20:23,960 --> 00:20:25,080
and then you're going to see

337
00:20:25,080 --> 00:20:26,320
the left pulmonary artery.

338
00:20:26,840 --> 00:20:29,260
On this cut not quite an arch

339
00:20:29,260 --> 00:20:30,860
but on further cuts

340
00:20:30,860 --> 00:20:33,120
if you kind of put those together

341
00:20:33,120 --> 00:20:34,060
as a stack of images

342
00:20:34,060 --> 00:20:35,540
you're going to see that mini arch

343
00:20:35,540 --> 00:20:36,460
that we can identify

344
00:20:36,460 --> 00:20:38,020
on the lateral view.

345
00:20:38,260 --> 00:20:41,210
On the right side

346
00:20:41,210 --> 00:20:43,190
we're going to look at a coronal image

347
00:20:43,190 --> 00:20:43,770
on the right

348
00:20:43,770 --> 00:20:45,630
and the sagittal reconstructed image

349
00:20:45,630 --> 00:20:46,430
on the left here.

350
00:20:47,810 --> 00:20:49,770
We can see the right pulmonary artery

351
00:20:49,770 --> 00:20:52,130
and notice its course on this coronal view

352
00:20:52,130 --> 00:20:55,470
so it branches from the main pulmonary artery

353
00:20:55,470 --> 00:20:59,110
and instead of arching anterior to posterior

354
00:20:59,110 --> 00:21:00,510
over the bronchus

355
00:21:00,510 --> 00:21:01,630
like the left side does

356
00:21:01,630 --> 00:21:03,370
the right pulmonary artery

357
00:21:03,370 --> 00:21:05,650
has to travel horizontally

358
00:21:05,650 --> 00:21:08,770
as it goes from left to right.

359
00:21:08,770 --> 00:21:10,030
So you can see

360
00:21:10,030 --> 00:21:11,690
based on the coronal orientation

361
00:21:11,690 --> 00:21:13,990
that on a lateral radiograph

362
00:21:13,990 --> 00:21:15,230
this hyaluropacity

363
00:21:15,230 --> 00:21:16,430
or the right pulmonary artery

364
00:21:16,430 --> 00:21:18,470
is actually going to be oval shaped

365
00:21:18,470 --> 00:21:19,910
and that pans out

366
00:21:19,910 --> 00:21:21,550
on the sagittal coronal

367
00:21:21,550 --> 00:21:23,310
or the sagittal reconstructed image

368
00:21:23,310 --> 00:21:24,510
on the left

369
00:21:24,510 --> 00:21:26,290
we see the oval shaped

370
00:21:26,290 --> 00:21:27,470
right pulmonary artery

371
00:21:27,470 --> 00:21:29,550
and then we're going to see

372
00:21:29,550 --> 00:21:31,470
the right upper lobe bronchus

373
00:21:31,470 --> 00:21:33,090
corresponding to

374
00:21:33,090 --> 00:21:36,010
what we see on a lateral radiograph

375
00:21:36,010 --> 00:21:40,820
The posterior tracheal stripe

376
00:21:40,820 --> 00:21:43,020
represents the tissues

377
00:21:43,020 --> 00:21:46,120
along the posterior tracheal margin

378
00:21:46,120 --> 00:21:48,820
which are intervening between gas

379
00:21:48,820 --> 00:21:50,460
within the trachea itself

380
00:21:50,460 --> 00:21:53,340
and in the adjacent right lung

381
00:21:53,340 --> 00:21:55,640
so let's take a look on the radiograph

382
00:21:55,640 --> 00:21:57,520
identify the trachea

383
00:21:57,520 --> 00:21:58,340
and then we can see

384
00:21:58,340 --> 00:22:00,180
the posterior tracheal margin

385
00:22:00,180 --> 00:22:02,240
is identified by this

386
00:22:02,240 --> 00:22:04,100
vertically oriented opacity

387
00:22:04,100 --> 00:22:06,420
so that's the posterior tracheal stripe

388
00:22:06,420 --> 00:22:08,300
On the left image

389
00:22:08,300 --> 00:22:09,900
we have an axial CT

390
00:22:09,900 --> 00:22:11,860
which shows

391
00:22:11,860 --> 00:22:13,800
just this little bit of soft tissue

392
00:22:13,800 --> 00:22:14,380
in this case

393
00:22:14,380 --> 00:22:15,980
the posterior tracheal wall

394
00:22:15,980 --> 00:22:18,000
outlined by gas

395
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:19,440
in the trachea anteriorly

396
00:22:19,440 --> 00:22:21,220
and outlined by the gas

397
00:22:21,220 --> 00:22:21,940
within the lung

398
00:22:21,940 --> 00:22:23,220
posteriorly

399
00:22:23,220 --> 00:22:25,280
I want you to notice

400
00:22:25,280 --> 00:22:26,580
that the esophagus

401
00:22:26,580 --> 00:22:29,140
is right adjacent to this

402
00:22:29,140 --> 00:22:30,460
and so sometimes

403
00:22:30,460 --> 00:22:31,480
that esophagus

404
00:22:31,480 --> 00:22:32,660
can be decompressed

405
00:22:32,660 --> 00:22:34,080
and pushed right up

406
00:22:34,080 --> 00:22:36,120
against the posterior tracheal wall

407
00:22:36,120 --> 00:22:37,460
so that not only

408
00:22:37,460 --> 00:22:38,060
are you seeing

409
00:22:38,060 --> 00:22:40,340
the posterior tracheal wall

410
00:22:40,340 --> 00:22:42,280
but also the esophagus

411
00:22:42,280 --> 00:22:43,800
so the normal thickness

412
00:22:43,800 --> 00:22:44,340
of this

413
00:22:44,340 --> 00:22:45,820
can be a little bit tricky

414
00:22:45,820 --> 00:22:48,200
without any intervening esophagus

415
00:22:48,200 --> 00:22:49,620
we usually use

416
00:22:49,620 --> 00:22:50,960
about 2.5 millimeters

417
00:22:50,960 --> 00:22:51,820
as kind of being

418
00:22:51,820 --> 00:22:52,940
the upper limit of normal

419
00:22:52,940 --> 00:22:54,060
but if you have

420
00:22:54,060 --> 00:22:55,420
the trachea plastered

421
00:22:55,420 --> 00:22:56,160
against the

422
00:22:56,160 --> 00:22:57,700
posterior tracheal wall

423
00:22:57,700 --> 00:22:58,380
and you're seeing

424
00:22:58,380 --> 00:22:59,940
both of these tissues

425
00:22:59,940 --> 00:23:02,000
then we typically allow

426
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:03,500
up to 5.5 millimeters

427
00:23:03,500 --> 00:23:04,920
so if you ever see this

428
00:23:04,920 --> 00:23:05,740
6 millimeters

429
00:23:05,740 --> 00:23:06,780
or more in thickness

430
00:23:06,780 --> 00:23:08,180
then you can be pretty

431
00:23:08,180 --> 00:23:09,200
confident

432
00:23:09,200 --> 00:23:09,760
that there's

433
00:23:09,760 --> 00:23:10,980
true thickening there

434
00:23:10,980 --> 00:23:19,190
this question

435
00:23:19,190 --> 00:23:20,090
tests your ability

436
00:23:20,090 --> 00:23:20,830
to recognize

437
00:23:20,830 --> 00:23:22,210
normal mediastinal borders

438
00:23:22,210 --> 00:23:23,270
on the lateral view

439
00:23:23,270 --> 00:23:24,850
the posterior

440
00:23:24,850 --> 00:23:26,370
and superior border

441
00:23:26,370 --> 00:23:26,890
of the heart

442
00:23:26,890 --> 00:23:27,770
on the lateral view

443
00:23:27,770 --> 00:23:29,610
represents the left

444
00:23:29,610 --> 00:23:30,170
atrium

445
00:23:30,170 --> 00:23:31,590
while the postero

446
00:23:31,590 --> 00:23:32,470
inferior border

447
00:23:32,470 --> 00:23:34,130
as shown in this question

448
00:23:34,130 --> 00:23:35,390
is formed

449
00:23:35,390 --> 00:23:37,210
by the left ventricle

450
00:23:37,210 --> 00:23:39,370
the right ventricle

451
00:23:39,370 --> 00:23:40,370
and the right ventricular

452
00:23:40,370 --> 00:23:41,470
outflow tract

453
00:23:41,470 --> 00:23:43,170
form the anterior border

454
00:23:43,170 --> 00:23:44,910
the right atrium

455
00:23:44,910 --> 00:23:46,370
is not border forming

456
00:23:46,370 --> 00:23:47,370
on the lateral view

457
00:23:47,370 --> 00:23:50,770
in this module

458
00:23:50,770 --> 00:23:52,390
we began our review

459
00:23:52,390 --> 00:23:53,130
of the normal

460
00:23:53,130 --> 00:23:54,490
mediastinal borders

461
00:23:54,490 --> 00:23:55,390
on both the frontal

462
00:23:55,390 --> 00:23:56,290
and lateral views

463
00:23:56,290 --> 00:23:56,870
of the chest

464
00:23:56,870 --> 00:23:58,590
in addition to

465
00:23:58,590 --> 00:23:59,470
mediastinal borders

466
00:23:59,470 --> 00:24:00,230
we talked about

467
00:24:00,230 --> 00:24:01,150
key stripes

468
00:24:01,150 --> 00:24:02,030
and spaces

469
00:24:02,030 --> 00:24:03,990
identified on these views

470
00:24:03,990 --> 00:24:05,170
including the

471
00:24:05,170 --> 00:24:06,650
azigoesophageal recess

472
00:24:06,650 --> 00:24:08,750
aortopulmonary window

473
00:24:08,750 --> 00:24:10,930
left paraspinal stripe

474
00:24:10,930 --> 00:24:13,450
right paratracheal stripe

475
00:24:13,450 --> 00:24:14,230
and then finally

476
00:24:14,230 --> 00:24:15,010
the posterior

477
00:24:15,010 --> 00:24:16,710
tracheal stripe

478
00:24:16,710 --> 00:24:18,130
it is important

479
00:24:18,130 --> 00:24:18,610
to be aware

480
00:24:18,610 --> 00:24:19,290
of the normal

481
00:24:19,290 --> 00:24:19,770
appearance

482
00:24:19,770 --> 00:24:20,530
of these spaces

483
00:24:20,530 --> 00:24:21,270
and stripes

484
00:24:21,270 --> 00:24:22,690
as pathology

485
00:24:22,690 --> 00:24:23,530
can alter

486
00:24:23,530 --> 00:24:24,450
this normal

487
00:24:24,450 --> 00:24:24,970
appearance

488
00:24:24,970 --> 00:24:26,930
finally we talked

489
00:24:26,930 --> 00:24:28,130
about osseous anatomy

490
00:24:28,130 --> 00:24:29,790
specifically about

491
00:24:29,790 --> 00:24:30,650
the ribs

492
00:24:30,650 --> 00:24:32,150
we also talked

493
00:24:32,150 --> 00:24:33,290
about the diaphragm

494
00:24:33,290 --> 00:24:34,590
and the costophrenic

495
00:24:34,590 --> 00:24:35,090
sulci

496
00:24:35,090 --> 00:24:36,450
on the frontal view

497
00:24:36,450 --> 00:24:39,780
we will complete

498
00:24:39,780 --> 00:24:40,920
our discussion

499
00:24:40,920 --> 00:24:41,960
of the radiographic

500
00:24:41,960 --> 00:24:42,440
anatomy

501
00:24:42,440 --> 00:24:43,580
of the chest

502
00:24:43,580 --> 00:24:44,260
in the second

503
00:24:44,260 --> 00:24:44,760
module

504
00:24:44,760 --> 00:24:45,780
of this unit

505
00:24:45,780 --> 00:24:47,600
this brings us

506
00:24:47,600 --> 00:24:48,240
to the end

507
00:24:48,240 --> 00:24:49,220
of this portion

508
